            <ArticleTitle>Assessing outcomes in body contouring.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>645-54</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.cps.2014.06.004</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0094-1298(14)00086-8</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Patient-reported outcome (PRO) instruments are questionnaires designed to measure outcomes of importance to patients from their perspective. This article describes the methods used to develop a new PRO instrument for obese patients and patients having bariatric and cosmetic body contouring surgery. The BODY-Q is composed of 19 newly designed scales that measure: (1) appearance; (2) health-related quality of life; and (3) process of care. Recommended guidelines for PRO instrument development were followed to ensure that the BODY-Q meets requirements of regulatory bodies. The BODY-Q is currently being field-tested in an international study.</AbstractText>
